/png\",\"sizes\":\"180x180\"}],[\"$\",\"$L64\",\"26\",{}]],\"error\":null,\"digest\":\"$undefined\"}\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"e:\"$9:metadata\"\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"66:I[49249,[\"9268\",\"static/chunks/aaea2bcf-143d3b0dc8132bc4.js\",\"4760\",\"static/chunks/4760-92886d3a47c56a0a.js\",\"5239\",\"static/chunks/5239-968bee541c792cd0.js\",\"1567\",\"static/chunks/1567-3788270a3266b680.js\",\"1029\",\"static/chunks/1029-cfe1019de973f2c5.js\",\"8024\",\"static/chunks/8024-a266353235ca72b5.js\",\"6405\",\"static/chunks/6405-dde0769e08ce9e4a.js\",\"4135\",\"static/chunks/4135-e8b62901ad46541c.js\",\"8754\",\"static/chunks/8754-47629ee0f92c664d.js\",\"9077\",\"static/chunks/9077-a5ff6a15c8f9d254.js\",\"2619\",\"static/chunks/2619-04bc32f026a0d946.js\",\"4127\",\"static/chunks/4127-35d78e9d1074522e.js\",\"9741\",\"static/chunks/9741-f019c2530cd37703.js\",\"1205\",\"static/chunks/1205-83775786d181d6d4.js\",\"1356\",\"static/chunks/1356-2ed480e29f4f7933.js\",\"9511\",\"static/chunks/9511-11a0e0265b2050b3.js\",\"5551\",\"static/chunks/5551-ecdab97733731a63.js\",\"6479\",\"static/chunks/6479-bed225d598e13aca.js\",\"7520\",\"static/chunks/7520-40c5336f15b4a173.js\",\"9909\",\"static/chunks/9909-d3a4bf3917b0dbbd.js\",\"8772\",\"static/chunks/8772-e628b0b54b9690f5.js\",\"9605\",\"static/chunks/9605-8b4af437952fd715.js\",\"327\",\"static/chunks/327-f32132b470154cde.js\",\"9819\",\"static/chunks/9819-16998e7b6612e64c.js\",\"8278\",\"static/chunks/8278-f2e8c2787066fac0.js\",\"9762\",\"static/chunks/app/article/%5B...doi%5D/page-90fbf624fba83569.js\"],\"default\"]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"32:[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"citation_keywords\",\"content\":\"diet; Jordan; Tyto alba; Bubo bubo; Bubo ascalaphus; Asio otus; Athene lilith; Strix aluco; Strix butleri\"}]\n65:T79e,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\u003cp\u003eBetween 2005 and 2015 I undertook eight trips to Jordan during which I collected pellets from seven owl species. In them 14,203 food items were identified. Mammals (Mammalia, 46 species, 37.9% of prey items) formed the most numerous component, invertebrates (Evertebrata) made up 33.4%, birds (Aves, 25.4%) were represented with at least 104 species, reptiles (Reptilia) came to 3.2%, and two species of amphibian were identified (Amphibia, 0.2%). Pharoah eagle owls (\u003cem\u003eBubo ascalaphus\u003c/em\u003e) and Byzantine eagle owls (\u003cem\u003eBubo bubo interpositus\u003c/em\u003e) primarily hunt larger mammals and birds, although Agamidae and Scorpiones were also represented more frequently among \u003cem\u003eB. ascalaphus\u003c/em\u003e. Mammals predominated among tawny owls (\u003cem\u003eStrix aluco wilkonskii\u003c/em\u003e) (Mammalia, 58.9%), mainly the eastern rock mouse (\u003cem\u003eApodemus mystacinus\u003c/em\u003e) (24.9%). For wintering long-eared owls \u003cem\u003eAsio otus otus\u003c/em\u003e the most important food was small birds (Aves, 78.3%), especially house sparrows (\u003cem\u003ePasser domesticus\u003c/em\u003e), Fringillidae and Sylviidae. For barn owls (\u003cem\u003eTyto alba erlangeri\u003c/em\u003e) the principal prey was small mammals (83.1%), mainly mice (\u003cem\u003eMus\u003c/em\u003e sp.), Günther’s vole (\u003cem\u003eMicrotus guentheri\u003c/em\u003e), grey hamster (\u003cem\u003eCricetulus migratorius\u003c/em\u003e) and shrews (Soricidae). Hume’s tawny owl (\u003cem\u003eStrix butleri\u003c/em\u003e) pellets contained mostly invertebrates (58.9%) and lizards, and their most frequent mammal prey were Wagner’s gerbil (\u003cem\u003eGerbillus dasyurus\u003c/em\u003e) and spiny mice (\u003cem\u003eAcomys\u003c/em\u003e sp.). There was an even higher propostion of invertebrates (86.4%) among lilith owlets (\u003cem\u003eAthene lilith\u003c/em\u003e). In addition to the insect orders Coleoptera, Orthoptera and Hymenoptera, remains of Scorpiones and Solifugae were also frequently found. The summarized results from individual owl species are compared with those gathered by the author in the surrounding Middle Eastern countries: Israel, Syria, Lebanon and Egypt.\u003c/p\u003e"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"33:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"abstract-en\",{\"name\":\"citation_abstract\",\"lang\":\"en\",\"content\":\"$65\"}]\n34:[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"citation_publication_date\",\"content\":\"2019/03/02\"}]\n35:[[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"citation_journal_title\",\"content\":\"Raptor Journal\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"citation_journal_abbrev\",\"content\":\"SRJ\"}]]\n36:[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"citation_publisher\",\"content\":\"Raptor Protection of Slovakia\"}]\n37:[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"citation_issn\",\"content\":\"2644-5247\"}]\n38:[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"citation_issn\",\"content\":\"1337-3463\"}]\n39:[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"citation_volume\",\"content\":\"12\"}]\n3a:[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"citation_issue\",\"content\":\"1\"}]\n3b:[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"citation_firstpage\",\"content\":\"9\"}]\n3c:[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"citation_lastpage\",\"content\":\"40\"}]\n3d:[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"citation_public_url\",\"content\":\"https://reference-global.com/article/10.2478/srj-2018-0003\"}]\n3e:[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"citation_abstract_html_url\",\"content\":\"https://reference-global.com/article/10.2478/srj-2018-0003?tab=abstract\"}]\n3f:[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"citation_pdf_url\",\"content\":\"https://reference-global.com/download/article/10.2478/srj-2018-0003.pdf\"}]\n40:[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"citation_fulltext_world_readable\",\"content\":\"\"}]\n41:[\"$\",\"$1\",\"author-0\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"citation_author\",\"content\":\"Ján Obuch\"}],[[\"$\",\"meta\",\"institution-0\",{\"name\":\"citation_author_institution\",\"content\":\"Comenius University in Bratislava\"}]],[\"$\",\"meta\",null,{\"name\":\"citation_author_email\",\"content\":\"obuch@rec.uniba.sk\"}],\"\"]}]\n42:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-0\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Amr ZS, Al-Melhim WN \u0026amp; Yousef MA 1997: Mammals remains from pellets of the eagle owl, Bubo bubo, from Azraq Neture Reserve, Jordan. Zoology in the Middle East 14: 5–10.10.1080/09397140.1997.10637698\"}]\n43:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-1\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Amr ZS, Handal EN, Bibi F, Najajrah MH \u0026amp; Qumsiyeh MB 2016a: Change in diet of the Eurasian eagle owl (Bubo b"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"ubo) suggests decline in biodiversity in Wadi Al Makhrour, Betlehem Governorate, Palestinian Territories. Slovak Raptor Journal 10: 75–80.10.1515/srj-2016-0002\"}]\n44:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-2\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Amr ZS, Robb MS, Nunes JMG, Baker MAA \u0026amp; Walsh A 2016b: Diet of the Omani owl, Strix butleri, near Nakhal, Oman. Zoology in the Middle East 62: 17–20.10.1080/09397140.2015.1132556\"}]\n45:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-3\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Amr ZS, Abu Baker MA, Qumsiyeh M \u0026amp; Eid E 2018: Systematics, Distribution and ecological analysis of rodents in Jordan. Zootaxa 4397: 1–94.10.11646/zootaxa.4397.1.129690341\"}]\n46:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-4\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Andrews IJ 1995: The birds of the Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan. Burns \u0026amp; Harris Limited, Dundec, Scotland.\"}]\n47:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-5\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Aulagnier S, Haffner P, Mitchell-Jones AJ, Moutou F \u0026amp; Zima J 2009: Mammals of Europe, North Africa and the Middle East. A \u0026amp; C Black Publishers, London.\"}]\n48:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-6\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Baker MAA, Qarqaz M \u0026amp; Amr ZS 2005: Diet of the barn owl (Tyto alba) in Shaumari Wildlife Reserve, Eastern Jordan. Jornal by the National Museum, Natural History Series 174, 1–4: 125–127.\"}]\n49:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-7\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Benda P, Andreas M, Kock D, Lučan R, Munclinger P, Nová P, Obuch J, Ochman K, Reiter A, Uhrin M \u0026amp; Weinfurtová D 2006: Bats (Mammalia: Chiroptera) of the Eastern Mediterranean. Part 4. Bat fauna of Syria: distribution, systematics, ecology. Acta Soc. Zool. Bohem. 70: 1–329.\"}]\n4a:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-8\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Benda P, Lučan RK, Obuch J, Reiter A, Andreas M, Bačkor P, Bohnenstengel T, Eid EK, Ševčík M, Vallo P \u0026amp; Amr ZS 2010: Bats (Mammalia: Chiroptera) of the Eastern Mediterranean and Middle East. Part 8. Bats of Jordan: fauna, ecology, echolocation, ecto-parasites. Ac"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"ta Soc. Zool. Bohem. 74: 185–353.\"}]\n4b:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-9\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Comay O \u0026amp; Dayan T 2018a: What determines prey selection in owls? Roles of prey traits, prey class, enviromental variables and taxonomic specialization. Ecology and Evolution 22: 3382–3392. DOI: 10.1002/ece3.3899.10.1002/ece3.3899586936229607033\"}]\n4c:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-10\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Comay O \u0026amp; Dayan T 2018b: From micromammals to paleoenvironments. Archeological and Anthropological Sciences 10: 2159–2171. DOI: 10.1007/s12520-018-0608-8.10.1007/s12520-018-0608-8\"}]\n4d:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-11\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Dor M 1947: Examining the diet of the barn owl. The Nature and the Land 7: 337–344.\"}]\n4e:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-12\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Göcer EG 2016: Diet of a nesting pair of long-eared owls, Asio otus, in an urban environment in south-western Turkey (Aves: Strigidae). Zoology in the Middle East 62: 1–4.10.1080/09397140.2015.1132561\"}]\n4f:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-13\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Harrison DL \u0026amp; Bates PJ 1991: The Mammals of Arabia, Second Edition. Harrison Zoological Museum, Kent.\"}]\n50:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-14\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Holliday T 2016: The book of frogs. A life size guide to six hundred species from around the world. Ivy Press, Lewes.10.7208/chicago/9780226184791.001.0001\"}]\n51:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-15\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"König C, Wieck F \u0026amp; Becking J-H 2015: Owls of the world, 2nd ed., Yale University Press, New Haven \u0026amp; London.\"}]\n52:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-16\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Mendelssohn HR \u0026amp; Yom-Tow Y 1987: Plants and animals of the land of Israel. An ilustrated encyclopedia, Appendix to volume 7: Mammals, Skull and body measurement and plates. Ministry of Defence Publishing House and Society for the Protection of Nature, Tel Aviv.\"}]\n53:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-17\",{\"name\":\"citation_refere"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"nce\",\"content\":\"Obuch J 2001: Using marked differences from the mean (MDFM) method for evaluation of contingency tables. Buteo 12: 37–46.\"}]\n54:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-18\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Obuch J 2011a: Diet of long-eared owl in the Middle East, 14–15. In: Ruzic M, Johnson DH \u0026amp; Paunovic K (eds), International conference on the survey, monitoring and conservation of the long-eared owl (Asio otus). Abstract book, Serbian owl conservation centre, Kikinda (Serbia).\"}]\n55:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-19\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Obuch J 2011b: Spatial and temporal diversity of the diet of tawny owl (Strix aluco). Slovak Raptor Journal 5: 1–120. DOI: 10.2478/v10262-012-0057-8.10.2478/v10262-012-0057-8\"}]\n56:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-20\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Obuch J 2014: Potrava výra skalného Bubo bubo v Jordánsku [Diet of the Eurasian eagle owl Bubo bubo in Jordan], 160–164. In: Manko P \u0026amp; Baranová B (eds), Zoológia 2014 \u0026amp; 19. Feriancove dni, Prešovská univerzita v Prešove, Prešov. [In Slovak with English abstract]\"}]\n57:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-21\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Obuch J 2017: Potrava sov v Jordánsku [Diet of owls in Jordan], 13–14. In: Kropil R \u0026amp; Lešo P (eds), Aplikovaná ornitológia 2017 – Zborník abstraktov, Technická univerzita vo Zvolene, Zvolen. [In Slovak]\"}]\n58:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-22\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Obuch J \u0026amp; Benda P 2009: Food of the barn owl (Tyto alba) in the Eastern Mediterranean. Slovak Raptor Journal 3: 41–50. DOI: 10.2478/v10262-012-0032-4.10.2478/v10262-012-0032-4\"}]\n59:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-23\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Obuch J \u0026amp; Krištín A 2004: Prey composition of the little owl Athene noctua in an arid zone (Egypt, Syria, Iran). Folia Zoologica 53: 65–79.\"}]\n5a:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-24\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Obuch J \u0026amp; Tulis F 2015: The diet of long-eared owl in Jordan: 183. In: Bryja J, Řehák Z \u0026amp; Zukal J"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1," (eds), Zoologické dny Brno 2015. Sborník abstraktů [Zoological days Brno 2015. Book of abstracts], Institute ofVertebrate Biology AS CR, Brno.\"}]\n5b:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-25\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Porter RF, Christensen S \u0026amp; Schiermacker-Hansen P 1996: Field guide to the birds of the Middle East. T \u0026amp; A. D. Poyser, London.\"}]\n5c:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-26\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Rifai LB, Al-Melhim WN \u0026amp; Amr ZS 1998: On the diet of the barn owl, Tyto alba, in northern Jordan. Zoology in the Middle East 16: 31–34.10.1080/09397140.1998.10637752\"}]\n5d:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-27\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Rifai LB, Al-Melhim WN, Ghoraibeh BM \u0026amp; Amr ZS 2000: The diet of the desert eagle owl, Bubo bubo ascalaphus, in the Eastern Desert of Jordan. Journal ofArid Environments 44: 369–372.10.1006/jare.1999.0601\"}]\n5e:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-28\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Shannon CE \u0026amp; Weaver W 1949: The mathematical theory of communication. University of Illinois Press, Urbana.\"}]\n5f:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-29\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Shehab AH 2004: Diet of the eagle owl, Bubo bubo, in Syria. Zoology in the Middle East 33: 21–26.10.1080/09397140.2004.10638060\"}]\n60:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-30\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Šipöcz T 2004: Zber. Databázový program, Verzia 3. [Collection. Database program. Version 3]. Botanical Garden, Comenius University, Blatnica. [In Slovak]\"}]\n61:[\"$\",\"meta\",\"reference-31\",{\"name\":\"citation_reference\",\"content\":\"Thevenot M 2006: Aperçu du régime alimentaire du Grand-duc d’Afrique du Nord Bubo ascalaphus à Tata, Moyen Draa. Go-South Bulletin 3: 28–30.\"}]\n67:T850,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\u003cabstract xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"\u003e\u003cp\u003eBetween 2005 and 2015 I undertook eight trips to Jordan during which I collected pellets from seven owl species. In them 14,203 food items were identified. Mammals (Mammalia, 46 species, 37.9% of prey items) formed the most numerous component, invertebrates (Evertebrata) made up 33.4%, birds (Aves, 25.4%) were represented with at least 104 species, reptiles (Reptilia) came to 3.2%, and two species of amphibian were identified (Amphibia, 0.2%). Pharoah eagle owls (\u003citalic\u003eBubo ascalaphus\u003c/italic\u003e) and Byzantine eagle owls (\u003citalic\u003eBubo bubo interpositus\u003c/italic\u003e) primarily hunt larger mammals and birds, although Agamidae and Scorpiones were also represented more frequently among \u003citalic\u003eB. ascalaphus\u003c/italic\u003e. Mammals predominated among tawny owls (\u003citalic\u003eStrix aluco wilkonskii\u003c/italic\u003e) (Mammalia, 58.9%), mainly the eastern rock mouse (\u003citalic\u003eApodemus mystacinus\u003c/italic\u003e) (24.9%). For wintering long-eared owls \u003citalic\u003eAsio otus otus\u003c/italic\u003e the most important food was small birds (Aves, 78.3%), especially house sparrows (\u003citalic\u003ePasser domesticus\u003c/italic\u003e), Fringillidae and Sylviidae. For barn owls (\u003citalic\u003eTyto alba erlangeri\u003c/italic\u003e) the principal prey was small mammals (83.1%), mainly mice (\u003citalic\u003eMus\u003c/italic\u003e sp.), Günther’s vole (\u003citalic\u003eMicrotus guentheri\u003c/italic\u003e), grey hamster (\u003citalic\u003eCricetulus migratorius\u003c/italic\u003e) and shrews (Soricidae). Hume’s tawny owl (\u003citalic\u003eStrix butleri\u003c/italic\u003e) pellets contained mostly invertebrates (58.9%) and lizards, and their most frequent mammal prey were Wagner’s gerbil (\u003citalic\u003eGerbillus dasyurus\u003c/italic\u003e) and spiny mice (\u003citalic\u003eAcomys\u003c/italic\u003e sp.). There was an even higher propostion of invertebrates (86.4%) among lilith owlets (\u003citalic\u003eAthene lilith\u003c/italic\u003e). In addition to the insect orders Coleoptera, Orthoptera and Hymenoptera, remains of Scorpiones and Solifugae were also frequently found. The summarized results from individual owl species are compared with those gathered by the author in the surrounding Middle Eastern countries: Israel, Syria, Lebanon and Egypt.\u003c/p\u003e\u003c/abstract\u003e"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"62:[\"$\",\"$L66\",null,{\"product\":{\"_id\":\"64738b264e662f30ba5416fa\",\"__v\":0,\"articleData\":{\"doi\":\"10.2478/srj-2018-0003\",\"journalKey\":\"SRJ\",\"volumeNumber\":\"12\",\"issueNumber\":\"1\",\"title\":{\"en\":\"\u003carticle-title\u003eOn the diet of owls (Strigiformes) in Jordan\u003c/article-title\u003e\"},\"isAccessible\":true,\"licenseType\":\"OpenAccess\",\"licenseText\":{\"en\":\"This work is licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 3.0 License.\"},\"copyright\":{\"en\":\"© 2018 Ján Obuch, published by Sciendo\"},\"ePubDate\":\"2019-03-02T00:00:00.000Z\",\"pPubDate\":\"2018-12-01T00:00:00.000Z\",\"receivedDate\":\"2018-05-28T00:00:00.000Z\",\"acceptedDate\":\"2018-09-11T00:00:00.000Z\",\"fPage\":\"9\",\"lPage\":\"40\",\"articleCategory\":null,\"prevArticle\":\"10.2478/srj-2018-0004\",\"nextArticle\":\"10.2478/srj-2018-0002\",\"pdfUrl\":\"https://reference-global.com/download/article/10.2478/srj-2018-0003.pdf\",\"keywords\":[\"\u003ckwd\u003ediet\u003c/kwd\u003e\",\"\u003ckwd\u003eJordan\u003c/kwd\u003e\",\"\u003ckwd\u003eTyto alba\u003c/kwd\u003e\",\"\u003ckwd\u003eBubo bubo\u003c/kwd\u003e\",\"\u003ckwd\u003eBubo ascalaphus\u003c/kwd\u003e\",\"\u003ckwd\u003eAsio otus\u003c/kwd\u003e\",\"\u003ckwd\u003eAthene lilith\u003c/kwd\u003e\",\"\u003ckwd\u003eStrix aluco\u003c/kwd\u003e\",\"\u003ckwd\u003eStrix butleri\u003c/kwd\u003e\"],\"classifications\":[],\"abstractContent\":{\"en\":\"$67\"},\"articleContent\":{\"en\":\"\"},\"figuresAndTables\":\"\",\"referenceList\":[{\"refId\":\"j_srj-2018-0003_ref_001_w2aab3b7b3b1b6b1ab1ab1Aa\",\"citeString\":\"Amr ZS, Al-Melhim WN \u0026amp; Yousef MA 1997: Mammals remains from pellets of the eagle owl, \u003citalic\u003eBubo bubo\u003c/italic\u003e, from Azraq Neture Reserve, Jordan. Zoology in the Middle East 14: 5–10.\u003cdgdoi:pub-id xmlns:dgdoi=\\\"http://degruyter.com/resources/doi-from-crossref\\\" pub-id-type=\\\"doi\\\"\u003e10.1080/09397140.1997.10637698\u003c/dgdoi:pub-id\u003e\",\"doi\":null,\"mixedCitation\":\"\u003cref id=\\\"j_srj-2018-0003_ref_001_w2aab3b7b3b1b6b1ab1ab1Aa\\\"\u003e\u003cmixed-citation\u003eAmr ZS, Al-Melhim WN \u0026amp; Yousef MA 1997: Mammals remains from pellets of the eagle owl, \u003citalic\u003eBubo bubo\u003c/italic\u003e, from Azraq Neture Reserve, Jordan. Zoology in the Middle East 14: 5–10.\u003cdgdoi: